## Lore

The Splugorth have long used Bio-Wizardry to empower their Minions and dominate their slaves. The Bio-Borg is a newer breed of Bio-Wizard transmutation and augmentation: a human or D-Bee slave given parasitic organisms, alien microbes, symbiotic unions and grafted limbs, combined into a nearly infinite variety of creatures. Most of these conversions are thought too dangerous and unreliable for loyal Minions, but more than acceptable for slaves, and they are performed every day. Humans and human-like D-Bees are the commonest subjects; the most suitable humans and ogres go to the Tattooed Man conversion instead, and the surplus becomes Bio-Borgs.

Whether called Elite, True or Full Bio-Borgs, these humanoids have undergone Bio-Wizard transmutation as well as being merged with symbiotes and other organisms or grafts. The process always changes their appearance and physiology, often into something far removed from human. They are made for military purposes (soldier, shock trooper, assassin, guard, spy, gladiator) or as the product of some experiment. The process is so all-encompassing that the subject is psychologically converted too: Bio-Wizard torture washes away the past life and its memories, and all skills, magic and psionics from that life are eliminated. Life begins anew as a first level Bio-Borg.

After conversion the slaves are trained, harshly, in their new skills and powers, then sold at the slave market. Most are sent to the arena or to war. The few who escape are hunted by Staphra or Slavers, shunned for their monstrous looks, and hunted by human supremacists like the Coalition. A Bio-Borg will not feel human, and most civilized peoples see them as monsters, the equivalent of evil demons.

Alignment: no restriction, but most are anarchist or evil; even once-good beings are transformed into anarchist or evil. No attribute requirements; a high P.E. and M.E. are recommended.

## Creating a True Bio-Borg

Choose one orientation (A to F), which names the tables to roll on and the special skills; or, for quick creation, pick one augmentation from each of the five tables, or roll on the Determination table. The G.M. may allow 1-3 more rolls or 1-3 more powers, but a True Bio-Borg should have no more than a dozen implants or features. It is possible to roll on the same table more than once; an incompatible result is re-rolled or replaced by a selection. Any short-lived organism is magically transmuted to last the Bio-Borg''s lifetime.

R.C.C. bonuses (full conversion): +1D4x10+12 S.D.C. (or +4D6+4 M.D.C. if a Mega-Damage creature), +4 P.S., +2 P.E., +1D6+2 Spd, +1 to roll with punch or impact, +2 to pull punch, +1 to strike, parry and dodge, +3 to save vs Horror Factor and +2 to save vs possession. Partial conversion: +5D6 S.D.C. (or +2D6+2 M.D.C. if a Mega-Damage creature), +1 P.S. and P.E., +1D4+1 Spd, +1 to roll with punch or impact, +1 to pull punch and +1 to parry and dodge.

## Partial Conversion

Anyone may receive one or two Bio-Wizard enhancements, symbiotes or parasites without becoming a Bio-Borg. A character who receives four or more enhancements starts life over as a Partial Bio-Borg: experience restarts at level one on the same table as the standard Bio-Borg, and all skills, magic knowledge and psionics from the past life stay frozen at the old level (P.P.E. or I.S.P. may be affected by the conversion). New skills are not gained with the new R.C.C.; once the Bio-Borg reaches his old level of experience he advances as usual. Controlling or inhibiting parasites alone do not make one a Bio-Borg; parasites that grant some measure of power do. A Partial Bio-Borg has no full transmutation: select two or three symbiotes, or two symbiotes and one or two parasites, from this book and/or Rifts Atlantis, usually no more than six in all. About 10% add a bionic limb and weapon and 1D4 mechanical implants.

## Insanity

At level one roll once on the Bio-Borg Insanity Table; at levels 3, 6, 10 and 14 roll percentile dice, and on 41-00 roll once more on the table or pick a new insanity. The table (printed 156-157): 01-10 Psychosis: Violent Rage; 11-20 Obsession: fighting and competition (loves it); 21-30 Obsession: danger (loves the thrill); 31-40 Obsession: Bio-Wizardry (hates it); 41-50 Obsession: sanitation (loves it); 51-55 Phobia: Bio-Wizardry (no initiative or combat bonuses against Bio-Wizards or their creations); 56-60 Phobia: undead (01-50% chance to flee; otherwise loses initiative and combat bonuses are halved); 61-65 Phobia: the Splugorth and their High Lords; 66-70 Phobia: sickness and disease (combat abilities halved when he believes himself sick, and magic and psionic illnesses inflict double penalties); 71-75 random affective disorder; 76-80 random neurosis; 81-85 random psychosis; 86-90 random phobia; 91-95 random obsession; 96-00 random insanity.

## Money and market value

Money: none. A loyal, trusted slave may be given an allowance of 2D4x10 credits a week; a runaway starts with no more than 1D6x100 in stolen credits or tradeable goods. Slave market value: 1D6x100,000 credits on average, double is common, and those with incredible powers can sell for as much as 1D4 million credits.

## Specific types

The Splugorth have developed a handful of True Bio-Borgs for specific combat and purposes: the Bio-Leech, Fire Eater, Kill-Crazy and Sym-Killer. The book does not describe them.
